    "leafs": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Maple Leafs",
      "league": "NHL",
      "record": "32-36-14",
      "detail": "8th in Atlantic Division",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "The season died with a thud, and not a dramatic one - just the slow, ugly unraveling of a club that never found a spine. <strong>Toronto's 32-36-14 finish, good for 78 points and 8th in the Atlantic, is a hard verdict, not a fluke</strong>. That's not a bad week; that's a broken year, with a 15th-place playoff seed and a seven-game losing streak leaving the sweater in the closet far too early. The Leafs spent the spring looking like a team between eras, never quite committed to the old core and not nearly bold enough to build the new one. The decisions ahead are the real headline now - coaching, roster surgery, and the uncomfortable question of who stays when the bill comes due. The front office can talk about retooling, but this was a club that bled points and lost its way in every tight stretch. <strong>What matters next is whether Toronto finally makes a ruthless summer move - or settles for another comforting press conference and the same old disappointment</strong>.",
        "updated": "Updated May 19, 2026"
      },
