    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "21-26",
      "detail": "3rd in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The Blue Jays keep finding new ways to lose the kind of games that harden a club - or expose it. <strong>Last night's 7-6 loss to the Yankees</strong> was the latest bruise, a game Toronto led by the look of things and still handed back, leaving it at 21-26, third in the AL East, 10.5 games off the pace and buried in the middle class of the American League at playoff seed 9. That's not a contender's profile; that's a team chasing its own shadow. The sting is that the bats have recently shown some life - four runs on May 17, two on May 16 - but the margin for error is shrinking by the night. One night it's a one-run grinder, the next it's a bullpen leak, and the record keeps telling the same blunt story. There's no mystery here: Toronto needs cleaner innings, crisper defense and somebody to grab the wheel when the game tilts. <strong>Tonight's rematch in New York will say whether this club can stop the slide - or keep feeding it.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 19, 2026"
      },
