      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Commanders",
        "body": "The draft dust has barely settled, and in Washington the question isn't who's coming - it's who can finally force their way onto the field. The Commanders sit third in the NFC East, 6 games back, with a 14 seed in the pecking order and a W1 attached to a season that is already over and now gives way to the real business: building a roster that can stand up in December, not just survive April. <strong>Washington's most interesting new name is Kaytron Allen, a sixth-round back out of Penn State, the kind of low-cost runner who can turn a crowded backfield into a meritocracy by August.</strong> That's the point of this class: competition, not ceremony. Allen arrives as a hammer with fresh legs, and in a league that chews up depth charts, the Commanders need runners who can protect the quarterback, catch the checkdown, and do something violent on third down. The rest of the offseason should be judged by one standard - who helps now, who helps later, and who gets swallowed by the numbers. <strong>Watch Allen in camp; if he pops, Washington may have found a useful piece before the leaves turn.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 19, 2026"
      },
